    "737131c5d278dd6b33c20af750a56f113089dcc7": {
      "sourceSetDigest": "9b201b21d619515351851254c2dd32b0f3b87648b3f635754465193b4ce92700",
      "sources": [
        {
          "sourceCommentId": "review-comment:4505551497",
          "sourceCommit": "234531cd838f21a18761e8ed1a00e48d4872781a",
          "sourceUrl": "https://github.com/splrad/steward/pull/304#discussion_r4505551497",
          "severity": "原文未说明",
          "bodyMarkdown": "## Duplicate 'if' condition\n\nThis condition is a duplicate of <a class=\"Link\" href=\"/splrad/steward/blob/234531cd838f21a18761e8ed1a00e48d4872781a/tools/quality-language/acceptance-fixture.js#L3-L3\">value === 'first'</a>.\n\n---\n\nTo fix duplicate conditions in an <code>if</code>/<code>else if</code> chain, ensure each condition is unique or remove unreachable duplicate branches.<br>\nIn this specific file (<code>tools/quality-language/acceptance-fixture.js</code>), the best non-speculative fix is to delete the duplicate <code>else if (value === 'first') return 'two';</code> line. This avoids inventing an unverified condition and preserves clear behavior: <code>'first'</code> maps to <code>'one'</code>, everything else maps to <code>'other'</code>.</p>\n<p>No new imports, methods, or definitions are needed.",
          "updatedAt": "2026-11-08T08:22:56Z"
        },
        {
          "sourceCommentId": "review-comment:4528313118",
          "sourceCommit": "737131c5d278dd6b33c20af750a56f113089dcc7",
          "sourceUrl": "https://github.com/splrad/steward/pull/304#discussion_r4528313118",
          "severity": "原文未说明",
          "bodyMarkdown": "## Duplicate 'if' condition\n\nThis condition is a duplicate of <a class=\"Link\" href=\"/splrad/steward/blob/737131c5d278dd6b33c20af750a56f113089dcc7/tools/quality-language/acceptance-fixture.js#L3-L3\">value === 'second'</a>.\n\n---\n\nTo fix a duplicate <code>if</code> condition without changing the function’s apparent intent, make each branch condition distinct so later branches can be reached when appropriate. Here, the safest minimal fix is to change the <code>else if</code> condition to a different value check.</p>\n<p>In <code>tools/quality-language/acceptance-fixture.js</code>, update line 4 from <code>else if (value === 'second') return 'two';</code> to <code>else if (value === 'first') return 'two';</code>. This preserves structure, keeps outputs stable for non-matching values (<code>'other'</code>), and removes unreachable code caused by the duplicate condition. No imports, helper methods, or additional definitions are needed.",
          "updatedAt": "2026-11-10T17:37:24Z"
        }
      ],
